Chapter: SerialBridge
  cites Foreword chapter CCE
  grounds Device.Port

 A way for the dev console to say something on a channel that is not the
 dev console. Every other inspector in this quire reports THROUGH the
 screen it is trying to tell you about, which is fine until the screen is
 the thing that is wrong; then the instrument and the subject fail
 together and the reading is silence. This chapter is the second wire.

 THE PORT IS THE ONE THE COMPILER ALREADY TALKS ON. `emit-serial-send-rdi`
 polls the line status register at 1021 for the transmit-holding bit and
 then writes the byte to 1016, and so does everything below. That is
 deliberate: it means a mirrored line arrives in the same stream a test
 harness already captures, so the mirror can be proven to have reached the
 wire rather than merely to have been asked to.

 A MIRROR MUST NEVER HANG THE THING IT MIRRORS, so the transmit wait is
 fuel-capped and a byte that cannot be sent is DROPPED and counted. The
 compiler's own emitter spins on that bit forever, which is right for a
 compiler whose only job is to finish printing and wrong for a debugger: a
 console that froze because nothing was draining COM1 would have turned a
 diagnostic aid into the fault. Every writer answers how many bytes it
 actually put on the wire, so a caller can tell a short write from a
 complete one instead of assuming.

 TEXT IS CCE INSIDE THE COMPILER AND THIS IS AN I/O BOUNDARY. `char-code`
 answers a CCE code point, not ASCII, so a byte taken straight from it
 would put `16 0d 15 11` on the wire for "derived". Every character goes
 through `to-unicode` here. This is the same conversion that was missing
 from a TLS hostname and every key-schedule label until 2026-07-13, and it
 is invisible to a round-trip test because both ends agree; the test for
 this chapter therefore reads the bytes back as the HARNESS sees them,
 which is the only reading that can tell the two apart.

 Anything that is not printable ASCII becomes `?`. A mirror is a byte
 channel and a debugger's audience is a terminal, so widening this to
 multi-byte output would buy nothing a reader could use, but the
 substitution is lossy and is named here rather than left to be
 discovered.

 THE READER IS NON-BLOCKING AND CARRIES AN EFFECT ROW FOR A REASON. A
 zero-argument definition is re-evaluated at every mention, and a read
 that CONSUMES the byte it returns would then quietly eat one byte per
 mention. Publishing `[Device.Port]` forces the caller to bind it once
 with an act-bind, which is the difference between a value and an event.

 We say:

Section: The Line

  sb-com1-data : Integer = 1016
  sb-com1-lsr : Integer = 1021
  sb-lsr-thre : Integer = 32
  sb-lsr-dr : Integer = 1
  sb-wait-fuel : Integer = 100000

 Answers the fuel left when the line went ready, or negative one when it
 never did. Returning the remaining fuel rather than a Boolean costs
 nothing and makes a nearly-exhausted wait visible to anyone who wants it.

  sb-wait-thre : Integer -> Integer
  sb-wait-thre (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then 0 - 1
    else if bit-and (port-in-byte sb-com1-lsr) sb-lsr-thre /= 0 then fuel
    else sb-wait-thre (fuel - 1)

Section: Writing

  sb-put-byte : Integer -> Integer
  sb-put-byte (b) =
    if sb-wait-thre sb-wait-fuel < 0 then 0
    else let w = port-out-byte sb-com1-data b
    in 1

 The CCE code point becomes a Unicode one and then a byte, and everything
 outside printable ASCII collapses to `?`. `to-unicode` answers negative
 one for a code point it has no mapping for, and that arm has to be
 handled here rather than trusted away: a carriage return is genuinely
 unmapped, and a raw negative reaching `port-out-byte` would write a
 byte nobody chose.

  sb-byte-for : Integer -> Integer
  sb-byte-for (cce) =
    let u = to-unicode cce
    in if u < 32 then 63
    else if u > 126 then 63
    else u

  sb-put-text : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sb-put-text (t) (i) (len) (n) =
    if i >= len then n
    else let b = sb-byte-for (char-code (char-at t i))
    in sb-put-text t (i + 1) len (n + sb-put-byte b)

  sb-write-text : Text -> [Device.Port] Integer
  sb-write-text (t) = sb-put-text t 0 (text-length t) 0

 The newline is written as a bare line feed and is COUNTED, so a caller
 comparing the answer against `text-length` is comparing against the
 wrong number by exactly one. That is stated because the alternative --
 not counting it -- would mean the function reports fewer bytes than it
 sent, and a drop and a deliberate omission would then look identical.

  sb-write-line : Text -> [Device.Port] Integer
  sb-write-line (t) =
    let n = sb-put-text t 0 (text-length t) 0
    in n + sb-put-byte 10

 A labelled line, because every caller in the console is about to build
 the same shape and a mirror stream with no labels is a wall of values.

  sb-write-field : Text, Text -> [Device.Port] Integer
  sb-write-field (label) (value) = sb-write-line (label & ": " & value)

Section: Reading

 Negative one means the line is idle. It is not an error and callers must
 not report it as one: an idle line is the normal answer on almost every
 poll.

  sb-read-byte : [Device.Port] Integer
  sb-read-byte =
    if bit-and (port-in-byte sb-com1-lsr) sb-lsr-dr == 0 then 0 - 1
    else port-in-byte sb-com1-data

 Whether a received byte ends a line. Both endings are accepted because
 both arrive: a terminal sends carriage return, a file redirected into the
 port sends line feed, and a caller that accepted only one of them would
 work for whichever was tried first and hang for the other.

  sb-is-eol : Integer -> Boolean
  sb-is-eol (b) = if b == 13 then True else b == 10

  sb-is-printable : Integer -> Boolean
  sb-is-printable (b) = if b < 32 then False else b < 127

Section: Assembling A Line

 THE CONVERSION RUNS THE OTHER WAY HERE AND IT IS THE SAME TRAP. A byte
 off the wire is ASCII; Text is CCE. Appending the byte directly would
 build a Text whose characters are numerically the ASCII values, and then
 `"mem"` typed down the serial line would not equal the `"mem"` the
 dispatcher compares against -- not because the comparison is wrong but
 because the two strings genuinely differ. Nothing would report an error;
 remote commands would simply never match, which is indistinguishable from
 a line that never arrived. `from-unicode` is what closes it, and it
 answers negative one for a byte it cannot map, which is refused rather
 than appended.

 These are pure and take no effect row on purpose: line assembly is
 ordinary text handling, and keeping it out of the reading function is
 what lets it be tested without a machine or a serial port.

  sb-char-for : Integer -> Text
  sb-char-for (b) =
    let cce = from-unicode b
    in if cce < 0 then "" else char-to-text (code-to-char cce)

  sb-append-byte : Text, Integer -> Text
  sb-append-byte (acc) (b) =
    if sb-is-printable b == False then acc
    else acc & sb-char-for b
